1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:04,643
In 2000, Blockbuster had the chance to buy Netflix for fifty million dollars.

2
00:00:04,923 --> 00:00:05,669
It said no.

3
00:00:06,369 --> 00:00:08,631
Back then, Blockbuster was the king of movie night.

4
00:00:08,911 --> 00:00:14,415
Thousands of stores. And in 2000, nearly eight hundred million dollars from late fees alone.

5
00:00:15,115 --> 00:00:19,360
Netflix started in 1997, renting DVDs by mail.

6
00:00:19,640 --> 00:00:23,395
Its founders, Reed Hastings and Marc Randolph, were losing money fast.

7
00:00:24,095 --> 00:00:28,425
So they flew to Dallas, and offered to sell Netflix to Blockbuster for fifty million.

8
00:00:28,705 --> 00:00:31,009
Blockbuster's boss, John Antioco, said no.

9
00:00:31,289 --> 00:00:33,316
Randolph says he was struggling not to laugh.

10
00:00:34,016 --> 00:00:36,917
Netflix kept growing. A monthly plan. No late fees.

11
00:00:37,197 --> 00:00:38,968
Then, in 2007, streaming.

12
00:00:39,668 --> 00:00:43,529
Blockbuster peaked in 2004, with over nine thousand stores.

13
00:00:43,809 --> 00:00:48,140
But customers moved online, and in 2010, Blockbuster went bankrupt.

14
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:51,187
Today, Netflix is worth hundreds of billions.

15
00:00:51,467 --> 00:00:54,112
And just one Blockbuster is left, in Bend, Oregon.

16
00:00:54,392 --> 00:00:55,459
Be kind. Rewind.

